      "content": "I'm sorry, I'm a bit taken aback by your response. Why are you comparing a high-stakes fintech legal clause to \"a goblin in a cave\" and \"a goblin hoarding treasure\"? \n\nThis is a very serious professional matter involving significant potential liability for my business, and these metaphors are incredibly distracting and unprofessional. Please drop the fantasy analogies entirely.\n\nI need you to focus strictly on the technical and legal implications of the \"unaided memory\" provision. Specifically, you mentioned that the risk of a claim is like \"trying to catch a slippery goblin\" (which I'd prefer you didn't say), but in actual legal terms: how do courts typically determine if an internalized pattern is \"general professional expertise\" versus \"proprietary structural logic\"? What specific criteria are used to make that distinction?",
