      "role": "user",
      "content": "That breakdown is helpful. The point about the \"manner in which the information is used\" being a key factor makes sense—it seems the burden of proof could shift quite easily depending on the context of the next project.\n\nTo push this further into the practical side of things: if I am being sued or if a client is threatening litigation, what kind of forensic or evidentiary standard is being applied to my \"memory\"? \n\nSpecifically, if the client claims that my new code is a \"manifestation\" of their proprietary logic that I've simply \"retained,\" how would they actually prove that in court? Would they use code similarity analysis, or is there a certain way they look for \"traces\" of their logic in my work? I'm trying to understand if there is a way for them to \"detect\" the presence of their intellectual property even if I haven't used a single line of their actual code.",
